WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.667
Training and alignment extras.

2
00:00:02.967 --> 00:00:08.012
Beyond the standard pipeline, here are the newer methods and the tooling that runs them.

3
00:00:08.012 --> 00:00:08.700
Eight terms.

4
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:16.433
GRPO, group relative policy optimization, is the RL method behind DeepSeek-R1.

5
00:00:16.733 --> 00:00:22.633
It samples a group of answers and scores them against each other, so no separate value model is needed.

6
00:00:22.933 --> 00:00:25.667
It's the current favorite for training reasoning ability.

7
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:28.867
These are the DPO family tree.

8
00:00:29.167 --> 00:00:33.667
ORPO folds preference optimization into SFT, so it's one stage.

9
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:37.867
KTO needs only thumbs up or thumbs down, instead of pairs.

10
00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:41.107
And IPO fixes an overfitting issue in DPO.

11
00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:44.467
Just know they're preference-alignment variants.

12
00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:48.433
Constitutional AI is Anthropic's method.

13
00:00:48.733 --> 00:00:53.933
The model critiques and revises its own outputs against a written list of principles.

14
00:00:54.233 --> 00:01:00.533
That's AI feedback, RLAIF, replacing much of the human labeling in RLHF.

15
00:01:01.733 --> 00:01:03.933
Both appear in RLHF.

16
00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:07.933
The reward model scores outputs by predicted human preference.

17
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:12.924
The KL penalty stops the policy drifting too far from the reference model.

18
00:01:12.924 --> 00:01:15.333
It's the leash against reward hacking.

19
00:01:16.533 --> 00:01:23.033
DoRA, weight-decomposed LoRA, splits weights into magnitude and direction, and adapts each separately.

20
00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:28.600
It usually beats plain LoRA at the same parameter budget, and it's a drop-in flag in peft.

21
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:36.833
Unsloth uses hand-optimized kernels to make LoRA and QLoRA fine-tuning about twice as fast and lighter.

22
00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:41.822
It's the default choice for solo-GPU fine-tuning in twenty twenty-six.

23
00:01:41.822 --> 00:01:44.300
Use it for your fine-tuning exercise.

24
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.100
These are config-driven fine-tuning frameworks.

25
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.833
You describe dataset, model, and method in YAML, and they run the whole job.

26
00:01:53.133 --> 00:01:58.367
Axolotl is the open-source community's workhorse, and Llama-Factory adds a web UI.

27
00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:01.567
This is Hugging Face's training stack.

28
00:02:01.867 --> 00:02:07.633
TRL implements the SFT, DPO, PPO, and GRPO trainers.

29
00:02:07.933 --> 00:02:14.300
Accelerate sits underneath and handles device placement, so one script runs on a laptop or a cluster.

30
00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:16.467
Let's recap.

31
00:02:16.767 --> 00:02:20.300
GRPO trains reasoning by scoring groups of answers.

32
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:26.733
The DPO variants and Constitutional AI handle alignment, and DoRA improves LoRA.

33
00:02:27.033 --> 00:02:31.267
Unsloth, Axolotl, and TRL are the tools that run the jobs.
